#### This experiment models length contraction by calculating how the measured length of an object decreases when observed from a stationary frame as it moves at relativistic speeds. The spatial dimension shrinks along the axis of motion.

### Python
The Python script calculates the Lorentz factor using the math module and applies it to the proper length of a moving object. It divides the proper length by the Lorentz factor to find the contracted length. The script outputs both lengths, demonstrating how the object appears compressed to a stationary observer.

### C
The C implementation calculates the contraction using the math.h library for the square root. It uses double-precision variables for the velocity fraction and the proper length. The program outputs the precise values of the length before and after contraction based on the object's speed.

### Rust
The Rust version uses floating-point primitives to perform the length contraction calculation safely. It computes the Lorentz factor using the square root method and applies it to the rest length. The script prints the contracted length, reflecting strict type safety during the mathematical operations.

### Shell
The shell script utilizes awk to calculate the Lorentz factor and the contracted length. It passes the initial length and velocity fraction to awk, which performs the relativistic math. The bash script then displays the results, showing how the length approaches zero as velocity approaches the speed of light.
